    "prompt": "Below is a description of the Python function `match_qualname`.\n\nDescription:\n\"\"\"\nThe `match_qualname` function is designed to create a match condition for a given qualified name (qualname) in the context of code refactoring or analysis. It leverages the `cst.metadata.QualifiedNameProvider` to retrieve the qualname metadata associated with a function or variable, rather than directly matching on the function name. This approach helps to handle issues related to scope and aliasing, such as when a function is imported with a different name using `from x import y as z`. The function takes a single parameter, `name`, which is the qualname to match against. It returns a `m.MatchMetadataIfTrue` object that encapsulates the matching logic. This object checks if all possible qualnames associated with a given node match the specified `name`. If there are multiple possible qualnames due to conditional imports, the function is conservative and only returns a match if all qualnames match the specified `name`. This avoids inadvertently breaking code during refactoring. The function does not handle errors or edge cases explicitly; instead, it relies on the underlying `cst` library to manage such scenarios. There are no side effects; the function is purely functional and does not modify any external state.\n\"\"\"\n\nSplit the description into atomic claims. Each claim must be a single, self-contained factual statement about the function that could be checked against the function's source code (name the function explicitly; no pronouns referring to other claims). Do not add claims that are not in the description; do not merge two facts into one claim. Keep the original wording where possible. Assign each claim exactly one type:\n- \"structural\": the function's signature, parameter names/defaults/types, return type, or which functions, methods, classes or attributes it uses.\n- \"dataflow\": how a value is computed, transformed, passed on, or what is returned